smbpal (0.2.12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * **macOS: sharing your own folders works.** `smbpal share add` publishes
    the folder as a macOS shared folder through the privileged helper, with
    guest access always off. Folders must be inside /Users or /Volumes, and a
    whole home folder is refused — share a folder inside it instead. File
    Sharing has to be on in System Settings; `smbpal status` says when it is
    not, and that turning it on serves every shared folder on the Mac.
  * **macOS: shares are served to accounts that already exist.** On a Mac the
    SMB password is the account's login password, so SMBPal does not set one:
    turn SMB sign-in on for the account in System Settings > General > Sharing
    > File Sharing > Options, and `smbpal status` says when it is off.
    `smbpal share make-writable` then lets that account write, with an access
    entry rather than by changing who owns the folder. Not yet: read-only
    shares, and `smbpal credential set` on macOS.
  * **macOS, upgrading from 0.2.11:** rebuild the helper from this release's
    source and install it with `sudo make -C helper install`, which also
    re-registers it with launchd. Replacing the binary on its own leaves the
    new helper unable to start.
  * Linux and macOS: `smbpal share add --user` refuses an account that does
    not exist, and says so before asking for a password. It used to accept
    it, and the share then looked healthy while nobody could open it.
  * Known on macOS: files a second account writes into a shared folder are not
    readable by the folder's owner, and a newly created account may need to
    log in once at the Mac before it can sign in over SMB.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:30:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * On macOS, trying to share a folder says so plainly instead of failing
    into Samba. It ended in `cannot write /etc/samba/smbpal.conf: Permission
    denied` — a path macOS has never had, naming a program it does not ship,
    suggesting a permission that would not have helped. And it got there
    *after* asking for an administrator password, which is the worst place to
    find out. It now answers before asking for anything, and says which half
    does work.
  * **A correction to 0.2.10.** That release's notes said a share "can be
    defined" on macOS and that only serving was missing. There is no such
    state, by design: SMBPal saves and applies as one step, because a saved
    share it has not applied would be a lie about what the machine is doing.
    So a share is either served or not recorded, and sharing folders on macOS
    does not work yet at all. Connecting to shares other machines serve does,
    and it works fully. Nothing on Linux was affected either way.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * On macOS, SMBPal can ask for permission, so it no longer refuses
    everything. The daemon was asking polkit -- which macOS does not have and
    never will -- and said so in its own startup log: every change to a share
    or a connection was refused, whoever you were. There is now a small
    privileged helper, installed once with `smbpal setup`, and changing
    anything asks for an administrator password the way the rest of the system
    does. Nothing changes on Linux, where polkit is still what answers.
  * On macOS, the daemon starts reliably when the machine does. Its socket
    lives in a directory only the privileged helper can create, and `/var/run`
    does not survive a reboot -- so if the daemon got there first it failed to
    start, and nothing brought it back for the rest of that boot. It now waits
    for that directory, and only for that: a daemon refusing to start because
    its configuration is broken still refuses, which is the point of it.
  * On macOS, being added to the `smbpal` group needs a new login session, and
    SMBPal says so now instead of telling you it does not. A shell that was
    already open keeps the groups it started with, and nothing you can ask
    from inside it will say otherwise.

  Defining a share on macOS is not the same as serving one yet: the part that
  tells macOS to serve it does not exist. Linux is unaffected by all of the
  above.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A connection you have just added now appears in your file manager
    straight away. It could be missed altogether: if you opened the share
    yourself within a few seconds of adding it -- which is the natural thing
    to do -- no icon appeared in the sidebar or on the desktop, and nothing
    brought it back until the next reboot. The faster you were, the more
    likely you were to lose it.
  * On macOS, `smbpal` finds its own daemon. It was looking for a socket in
    `/run`, a directory macOS does not have and will not let anything create,
    so every command answered that no daemon was listening. Nothing changes
    on Linux, where that path is correct.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:20:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Macs now appear under their real names. A name with any character outside
    plain ASCII was being mangled -- an apostrophe became three symbols -- and
    since macOS calls a machine "<Name>'s MacBook Air" by default, that was
    very nearly every Mac on the network.
  * You can connect to shares SMBPal used to refuse. A share name containing
    an apostrophe or a dollar was rejected for a reason that applies only to
    shares this machine serves, not to shares it mounts. That blocked the
    folder every Mac shares by default, and every `...$` share on Windows.
  * New: `smbpal browse <host>` lists the shares a server offers, so a name
    no longer has to be guessed. It needs a username on some servers --
    macOS refuses to list anything to an anonymous caller.
  * `smbpal connection add` now says so when the server has no share by the
    name you gave, and prints the ones it does have, instead of saving the
    typo and failing to mount days later.
  * New: `smbpal connection set-credentials <ref> --user <name>` changes the
    password on a connection that already exists. Until now a password could
    be set only when the connection was created, and correcting one meant
    deleting the connection and typing it all again.
  * This release depends on libsmbclient, which is what lists a server's
    shares without putting a password on a command line or in a file.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:40:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * macOS can mount a share, end to end, against a real server. On Linux this
    release changes nothing you can see.
  * The daemon asks your own agent to do the mounting. It connects to a socket
    in your home directory and the agent checks which user is on the other end
    before it acts, so root never holds the password and never reaches into
    your Keychain.
  * The password goes into your login Keychain, and the agent reads it back
    from there. macOS will not consult the Keychain on behalf of a mount that
    is forbidden to ask you anything, so SMBPal looks the password up itself.
  * macOS picks the mountpoint, not SMBPal. A share named `Testshare` arrives
    at `/Volumes/Testshare`, because `/Volumes` belongs to root and an agent
    cannot prepare a directory there -- so SMBPal records where the share
    landed instead of deciding beforehand.
  * Every mount asks for a fresh session. macOS caches a server's credential
    somewhere SMBPal cannot clear, which made a corrected password look broken
    and a deleted one look as though it were still in use.
  * Two macOS messages stopped misleading. Disconnecting says what it did once
    rather than twice; and a machine that is serving a share is no longer
    reported unreachable, which it never was -- macOS refuses local network
    access to a program that has not been granted it, and SMBPal cannot ask
    for that yet, so it now says nothing rather than something false.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sat, 03 Oct 2026 10:20:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * macOS groundwork. On Linux this release changes nothing you can see,
    except that the package now ships an `smbpal-agent` command which exists
    for macOS and says so when it is run here.
  * Mounting on macOS is implemented, and it runs in a per-user agent rather
    than in the daemon. Two measurements put it there: mounting on macOS
    needs no elevation at all, and the login Keychain -- where the password
    belongs -- cannot be read by root.
  * The agent installs itself with `smbpal-agent --install`, which writes a
    LaunchAgent and loads it. `--status` says whether launchd has it, is
    retrying it after a failure, or is pointing at a program that has moved,
    because `launchctl` reports all three the same way.
  * `smbpal` no longer tells a Mac user to run `systemctl`. There is no
    SMBPal daemon on macOS yet, and naming a command the platform does not
    have sent people looking for something they could not install.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:50:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Three messages say what they mean. Each was accurate and each named a
    symptom that several different causes share, so it read as though one
    cause had been identified and sent people to the wrong fix.
  * A refused connection now names both of the things that cause one. A
    server refusing a connection gives no reason, so SMBPal could not tell
    a stopped share from a blocked port -- and the two are fixed in
    opposite ways. It now says so, and points at `smbpal status` on the
    other machine, which can tell.
  * Disconnect says what it did. Disconnecting unmounts the share and
    leaves it ready to mount again the moment anything opens the folder,
    which on some desktops happens immediately -- so the button appeared to
    do nothing at all. The window now says what happened.
  * `smbpal browse` no longer says a machine is not running SMBPal when it
    is. The column reported whether a machine was sharing something at that
    moment, under a heading that promised something else, and answered "no"
    for the computer you typed the command on. It is now headed SHARING
    NOW.

